- OS will call the main function(int main()), return 0 at the end of the program takes back to OS itself.
- statements mentioned after return statement in function definition are not considered.
Context Switching:
- after mentioning #include <stdio.h> the code directly goes to int main() {}.
- whenever the function call is mentioned in the main block of code, then function definition block of code is executed.
- when ever it reaches the return statement in function block, it comes back to main block to function call.
- then the values of the function call gets updated.
- then the output gets printed.
- This process is called "Context Switching".

MEMORY SEGMENT:
- Many processes can be done in user space, which is taken care by the OS.
- Memory is divided into  segments
1) Stack
2) Heap
3) Data
4) Code

- Heap is used to store the dynamic memory.
- Constants are stored in Code.
- Local variables(int i, int n, etc) and function memory(whatever inside the function block) is stored Stack.

STACK FRAME:
- Main block anf function block both has sepearte stack frames
- once the output is printed(when in both blocks reaches its return statements), both the stack frames are automatically deleted.
- If we give new inputs , then new stack frames will be created.
- Address of both stack frames is also different.
- So no need to give same variables in both blocks.
- output is considered based on the function_name,so it should be mentioned same in both blocks.
- Stack frame consists of:
1) Local variables - int i, int n, etc
2) Return Address 
- Address of the function block is main, because main block is calling the function.
- Address of the function block is OS, because OS is calling the main block to execute.
3) Parameters List(PL) - Inputs of funtion(n1, n2, etc)
- refer notes for more examples.

- If we are mentioning void as return datatype, the no need to mention return keyword in function block. we can give printf statement here itself.
eg:
#include <stdio.h>
void even_odd(int n)
{
    if(n%2 == 0)
    printf("Even\n");
    else
    printf("Odd\n");
}
int main() {
    int n, res;
    scanf("%d", &n);
    even_odd(10);
    return 0;
}

output: Even

